From 4913742a9a170a59fa28d17df95cef6c9c00de5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 08:34:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 095/101] sunxi: h713: hand the projector its own HDCP 1.4 key
 set

The display firmware polls the receiver's "key loaded" bit in Rx_HDCP14_LoadKey
and gives up early, so the key set has to be in the sink before the firmware is
released - userspace is far too late. This reads the 288-byte key set out of this
device's own secure storage and lets our EL3 service run one Crypto Engine task
that moves it into the sink at 0x03041400. The key set never becomes readable:
the engine decrypts it with a key that exists only inside the SoC and writes it
straight into the receiver.

Three measured rules shape the code, and none of them may be "simplified" away:

  - key-select 3 towards readable DRAM is refused with ESR 0x11. That is the
    design, not a fault: such a task would be key export.
  - the DMA source must lie in the Trusted OS window, so the bytes are staged at
    0x48a00000 first. This was the one thing missing for hours, because the
    engine answers the same refusal either way.
  - the push must happen after the TV domain is powered. Before that the task
    completes with ESR 0 and latches nothing.

No unwrapping is needed: the vendor's key box only decrypts items whose
"encrypted" flag is set, and here op 1 builds no decrypt task at all. The stored
bytes are byte-identical to the source of the vendor's own task (0 of 288
differ), so they go to the sink unchanged and no wrapping key is needed anywhere.

The item is found by magic, name and length - never by a constant offset - and
both stored copies must agree before anything is pushed. Every failure prints one
line and returns: a device without a usable key set still boots, just without
protected playback. A watchdog guard sits around the push, armed only if nobody
else is driving the watchdog, so a wedged engine resets the board instead of
parking it at the logo.

Proven at the picture, with a cleared sink as the control, because the sink
survives a warm reset and an "Enabled" after an earlier push proves nothing:
wipe -> source reports Desired -> push -> source reports Enabled.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
